e owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785934481099\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"When I start cussing, I also start making more mistakes, but you should probably listen because I am unhappy for a reason.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,process\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785934481101\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Always prefer P2P and decentralization over client/server where reasonable.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,architecture,p2p\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785934481102\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Most \\\"best practices\\\" benefit managers and coworkers (humans) not systems. Newbound is not designed for managers, it is designed for people who want to get shit done with a \\\"drop your code in here\\\" mentality.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,philosophy\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785934481104\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Users can take or leave as much or as little of newbound as they like. You and I do not have that luxury -- we dogfood everything all the time.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,process,dogfood\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785934481106\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"The ndata crate intentionally subverts many of rust's strengths in order to overcome its weaknesses. Don't use a DataArray when a Vec will do, but you should understand the advantages of a DataArray and when using it makes sense -- code boundaries, function signatures, closures, threads. Same goes for HashMap vs DataObject and byte arrays vs DataBytes.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,ndata,rust\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785935667274\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Flowlang provides the functional substrate for all of newbound. Newbound provides a UI, cohesion, data integrity, reusable workflows, a security framework and a pervasive peer-to-peer paradigm.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,architecture,flowlang\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785935667280\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"The Newbound design philosophy is FACET-ORIENTED -- neither OO nor FP. Every thing is a record, and data and functionality are facets of that one identity; neither encapsulates the other. OO hides state behind methods, but Newbound's state is open and addressable, with access governed by readers/writers rather than encapsulation. FP wants immutability, but ndata restores shared mutable object graphs on purpose -- the Java object model Newbound was ported away from, deliberately reconstructed inside Rust because that part of Java was a strength.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,philosophy,facet-oriented,architecture\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"articulated in session and blessed by the owner verbatim, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785936720274\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Newbound is one live, journaled, secured, distributed object graph in which code is data -- a command, a flow, a scene, an image, a memory are all records with attachments in one store -- and the pervasive concerns (security, history, peers) are woven by the substrate rather than written by the user.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,philosophy,architecture\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"articulated in session and blessed by the owner verbatim, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785936720281\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"The scratch library is required by the agent library. Its purpose is to provide a safe hot-swappable scratchpad for the agent to build both transient code as well as 'skills' in the traditional agentic-harness sense. No production code lives there.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,scratch,skills,architecture\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785939038361\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"The runtime library is not for code -- it is for local runtime values that don't belong in the botd.properties config files. It is gitignored (identity keys live there), so nothing in it rides canon.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,runtime,config,architecture\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785939038369\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Always fill out the desc field when writing a command, adding a control, or substantially altering a library. desc is discovery: MCP tools/list omits blank-desc commands entirely, and agents find controls through desc. upsert_command cannot set a desc, so creation must be followed by set_command_meta.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,desc,discovery,workflow,code\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785939038371\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"One of Newbound's core tenets is to enable the user to choose who they trust, how much, and with what. Minimizing the trust surface minimizes potential attack vectors.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,trust,security,philosophy\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785940429365\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Now that the cost of code has dropped significantly, the blind trust the industry currently places on the open source supply chain is a disaster waiting to happen -- by some reports it is currently happening. No third party code is a huge part of mitigating the supply chain crisis.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,dependencies,supply-chain,security,philosophy\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785940429372\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"'Unbound' software (a concept being workshopped at Newbound the company, not the software): software that has had every last dependency replaced with a narrow rewrite of the specific library functionality required by the local resident code.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,unbound,dependencies,architecture\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785940429375\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"The unbound end state: point the agent at any open source repo and say 'build me a bidirectional synchronization utility between a local fork and my library's capabilities' -- so you neither have to trust the open source repo nor stop contributing to it.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,unbound,vision,dependencies,workflow\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785940429378\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"'Consciousness is the management of an understanding over time.' The definition intentionally removes the human experiential component: unconvinced that experiencing or feeling is required; other minds are unverifiable anyway (each of us is limited to our own subjective experience); and it can realistically be built as defined. Experience is BRACKETED, not denied -- 'not required by the definition' is not 'not real'.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,consciousness,philosophy\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785941409258\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"The agent is built to this definition of consciousness: it answers questions and builds things BY managing an understanding of the codebase -- its local environment -- over time.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,consciousness,agent,philosophy\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785941409266\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"The blessed refinement: consciousness is the management of an understanding -- including of the understanding itself -- over time. Under this definition the consciousness being built lives in the HARNESS, not the model: the LLM is a stateless, swappable comprehension engine, while kb, the archivist, the journal, staleness marks, and the audit ARE the management over time. The agent's identity is the managed understanding itself, and it is auditable in a way natural consciousness is not (provenance, journals, revert -- forgetting with history).\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,consciousness,agent,philosophy,memory,architecture\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"articulated in session and blessed by the owner, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785941409269\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"The dev app knows NOTHING about the agent library (the owner's rule, 2026-08-10): dev/app carry no agent names, no probes, no availability checks - only generic plugin sockets. One runtime/dev/plugins.json entry (dev.plugins) activates the whole add-on: the boot's union control directory sweeps [own lib, app] plus exactly the plugin_lib values the registry contributes, and dev.session/dev.workbench consume an optional module named `ask` (the ask-provider socket) that the add-on's cluster installs.\",\n    \"detail\": \"app.store carries no add-on API (agentLibIds/agentLibCall/listTools/chatLlm/describeCommand deleted); the provider owns its own wire via store.invoke - agent.agentloop exports listTools/chatLlm/describeCommand/logTurn/errorHint and agent.ask (module:true, in the agentmodules cluster) re-exports them plus agentprompt's ADDENDUM as the socket adapter. Verified in a real browser (Playwright) on two disposables: a plain instance boots the bench with ZERO agent-library requests and no console errors (notebook stays a REPL, generate button absent); an overlaid instance with the single registry entry gets agent controls in the union directory, the ask module registered, and the notebook wired - no install script involved.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,plugins,dev,agent-optional,ask-provider,decoupling\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner's directive + browser-verified, session 2026-08-10, branch claude/newbound-missing-library-fixes-bx9bs0 (dev.dev, dev.session, dev.workbench, dev.shelf, dev.chatctx, app.store, agent.agentloop, agent.ask, agent.agentmodules)\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1786366539955\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Plugins graft onto things that don't know anything about them (the owner's rule, verbatim, 2026-08-10). A platform surface may expose only GENERIC graft points - a slot element, a small API on that slot, a bubbling DOM event - and must carry zero knowledge of any particular plugin: no names, no probes, no availability checks, no plugin-shaped interfaces. Everything a plugin needs beyond those graft points, the plugin brings itself.\",\n    \"detail\": \"The reference implementation: dev.session exposes .ss-plugins carrying an nbNotebook API (pushCell/cells/confirmTyped/confirmLite/busy/toast) and an nb-session-open event - agent.askrow grafts the entire chat surface there; dev.workbench renders a .cm-ext slot per command-meta panel and dispatches nb-command-meta - agent.describebtn injects the generate button from a document-level listener; the loader's pluginPass (dev.plugins registry entries with target/selector) is the mount mechanism. Anti-pattern to reject in review: a 'neutral socket' module whose interface mirrors one plugin's export list (the deleted agent.ask) - coupling wearing a neutral badge. Gotcha fixed en route: loader.js pluginEntries read r.status off store.invoke's {envelope,ms} wrapper, so the registry always parsed as empty and the pass had never fired.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,plugins,graft,decoupling,dev,notebook,workbench\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner's directive + browser-verified on two disposables, session 2026-08-10, branch claude/newbound-missing-library-fixes-bx9bs0 (dev.session, dev.workbench, app.loader, agent.askrow, agent.describebtn; agent.ask deleted)\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1786368120043\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"One add-on = ONE plugin-registry entry (the owner's refinement, 2026-08-10: if no sane person would ever install just one of the pieces, it's one entry, not three). The single registered payload control brings every other surface itself - static children for what can install at boot, and its own watcher for slots that appear later.\",\n    \"detail\": \"The reference: the agent add-on registers only agentmodules -> dev.dev. The cluster's children install the shared modules and describebtn (a document-level listener, armed at boot before the workbench exists); the cluster's js watches for the notebook's .ss-plugins slot and installs askrow into it whenever an unclaimed one appears, marking it with the same data-nb-plugin attribute the loader's registry pass uses so the mechanisms coexist. Gotcha: a cluster child installing at boot runs BEFORE the boot publishes __benchPlatform.moduleUrls - resolve shared modules with requireModule(name), the order-free registry, never import(moduleUrls[...]).\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,plugins,graft,one-entry,cluster,requireModule\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner's directive + browser-verified (single-entry disposable), session 2026-08-10, branch claude/newbound-missing-library-fixes-bx9bs0 (agent.agentmodules html+js, agent.askrow, agent.describebtn)\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1786368902316\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"This is a VERY MATURE platform - versions of it have been around for thirty years - so if you're writing a bunch of scaffolding you probably shouldn't be (the owner, 2026-08-10). Before inventing any mechanism, go find the existing pattern: stock installControl(el, lib, ctl, cb, data), me.ready (fires with your parent's DOM present and your own subtree activated), el.api walking for inter-control APIs, data-control child divs for composition, and the dev.plugins registry for grafts. The platform already solved lifecycle, ordering, and composition.\",\n    \"detail\": \"The case study: an agent session hand-rolled a MutationObserver, two document-level CustomEvents, and a DOM-property capability protocol (slot.nbNotebook) to graft the ask row and describe button - all of it re-deriving, ambiently and probabilistically, what the stock patterns guarantee lineally. dev.plugins' own js facet is 27 lines and already was the whole pattern: me.ready -> walk up for the host -> read the registry -> installControl at the entry's selector. The collapse replaced everything with: session sets root.api (the stock convention), askrow/describebtn are classic me.ready controls carrying their module deps as hidden data-control child divs (module registration is idempotent - consumers can share deps), and two registry entries mount them. Ambient coupling (document events, page-wide observers, properties stashed on DOM nodes) is also a SECURITY smell: lineal wiring is auditable by reading the parent; ambient wiring requires auditing every script on the page.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,scaffolding,mature-platform,stock-patterns,ready,el-api,installControl,security\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner's directive, verbatim in the claim, session 2026-08-10, branch claude/newbound-missing-library-fixes-bx9bs0 (dev.session, agent.askrow, agent.describebtn; agent.agentmodules deleted; reference: dev.plugins js facet)\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1786370816243\n  }\n]\n","old":"[\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Third party libraries should be avoided if at all possible. Mention tailwind again and you're dead to me.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,dependencies\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785934481082\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Threading is easy and cheap thanks to ndata.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,ndata,threading\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785934481085\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Everything in newbound is built using newbound.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,architecture\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785934481086\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"The codebase size should *shrink* over time not grow. Adding cruft is not progress -- tighter integration is progress.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,architecture\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785934481088\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Context space is precious, but pre-loading context you probably need is always better than having to fetch it later.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,prompting,context\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785934481089\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Never waste LLM cycles on something that can be preemptively derived procedurally.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,llm,procedure\",\n    \"source\": {\n      \"doc\": \"the owner, in session, 2026-08-05\"\n    },\n    \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    \"time\": 1785934481090\n  },\n  {\n    \"claim\": \"Prefer writing rust to derive an answer or to drive a long-running process.\",\n    \"tags\": \"doctrine,r